  {
    question: 'What constellations belong to the Ursa Major family?',
    answer: 'The Ursa Major family is a group of ten constellations occupying the northern sky around the celestial pole: Ursa Major, Ursa Minor, Draco, Canes Venatici, Boötes, Coma Berenices, Corona Borealis, Camelopardalis, Lynx, and Leo Minor. They are grouped by their shared location in the northern heavens rather than by a single myth, and several of them are circumpolar from mid-northern latitudes.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is the Big Dipper a constellation?',
    answer: 'No — the Big Dipper is an asterism, not a constellation. It is a pattern of seven bright stars that forms the most recognizable part of the constellation Ursa Major, the Great Bear. The dipper makes up the bear’s hindquarters and long tail. Because it is so easy to spot, the Big Dipper serves as the gateway to the entire northern sky, pointing the way to Polaris and other constellations.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do you find Polaris using the Big Dipper?',
    answer: 'Locate the two stars at the end of the Big Dipper’s "bowl," farthest from the handle — Dubhe and Merak, known as the Pointers. Draw an imaginary line from Merak through Dubhe and extend it about five times the distance between them. That line leads to Polaris, the North Star, which marks the north celestial pole and sits at the tip of the Little Dipper’s handle in Ursa Minor.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the brightest star in the Ursa Major family?',
    answer: 'Arcturus, in Boötes, is the brightest star in the family and the fourth-brightest star in the entire night sky. It is a red-orange giant about 37 light-years away. You can find Arcturus by "arcing" along the curve of the Big Dipper’s handle and continuing that arc — the mnemonic is "arc to Arcturus." Continuing the same arc onward leads to Spica in Virgo ("spike to Spica").'
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is Ursa Major important for stargazing?',
    answer: 'Ursa Major is one of the most useful constellations because, from most northern locations, it is circumpolar and therefore visible on any clear night of the year. Its Big Dipper asterism acts as a natural signpost: its stars point not only to Polaris but also, by extending various lines, to constellations including Leo, Boötes, and Cassiopeia. Learning the Big Dipper first is the classic entry point into practical astronomy.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What deep-sky objects can be found in this family?',
    answer: 'The Ursa Major family is rich in galaxies because it looks away from the crowded plane of the Milky Way. Highlights include the Whirlpool Galaxy (M51) in Canes Venatici, the Pinwheel Galaxy (M101) in Ursa Major, the bright pair Bode’s Galaxy (M81) and the Cigar Galaxy (M82), and the Sunflower Galaxy (M63). Coma Berenices contains the Coma Star Cluster and a portion of the vast Coma–Virgo galaxy cluster, making this region a favourite for galaxy-hunters.'
  }
